# Copy to .env (gitignored). mise loads it into your shell, so pnpm scripts, drizzle-kit and tests see it.
# Validated in packages/env — keep in sync.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/proof
# apps/hq only (the org brain): its own database, with the same migrations applied. HQ_BASE_URL
# defaults to http://127.0.0.1:<port>.
# HQ_DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/proof_hq
# HQ_BASE_URL=
# The founders: only they decide hq proposals, edit entries, grant spend and click Approve in Slack.
# Comma-separated <email>=<slack user id> (or id:<user id>=<slack user id>); an email counts once its
# account has verified it, and only in local and e2e stacks (NODE_ENV development or test: `pnpm
# --filter @proof/hq dev` sets it; `start` does not, so it is a deployed hq). A deployed
# hq takes id: entries only: make the account with `pnpm --filter @proof/hq create-founder --email <e>
# --slack-id <U..> --password-out <file>`, which prints the id: entry to add. hq writes them to its
# founders table at every boot and removes the rest, so this list is the only way to add or remove a
# founder; empty, nobody is one. After changing it, restart hq and regenerate the agent's list (below);
# `pnpm hq:predeploy` checks the two agree.
# HQ_FOUNDERS=
# The Slack workspace (team id) whose clicks hq accepts, and the Slack app's signing secret (hq and the
# agent both verify a click with it). Either unset and hq refuses every Slack click.
# HQ_SLACK_TEAM_ID=
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=
# The most Slack grant clicks may add to the day's allowance in total, in dollars (UTC day; default 50,
# at most 1000). A click that would pass it is refused and grants nothing; the request stays pending.
# HQ_SLACK_DAILY_GRANT_CAP_USD=
# Sign-up on hq is closed unless this is true, and only the e2e and test stacks (NODE_ENV development
# or test) may turn it on: a deployed hq refuses to boot with it, and create-founder refuses to run.
# Make a deployed hq's founders with create-founder instead. A password reset that reclaims an
# unverified account disables the keys and OAuth grants it held before.
# HQ_ALLOW_SIGNUP=
# Optional: a database named *_test for hq's real-Postgres spend race test (a worktree's DATABASE_URL
# already works). The test writes and deletes rows dated in 2200, so never point it at real data.
# HQ_TEST_DATABASE_URL=
# apps/agent only (the eve agent; read through @proof/env/agent, never validated with the product's
# env). It reaches hq at HQ_BASE_URL (above) with an hq API key that has the agent's scope only: read
# all, propose, runs and spend (hq_entry:read, hq_proposal:read+create, hq_run:*, hq_spend:read+record+request),
# from the org's vault (made on the server with `pnpm --filter @proof/hq mint-agent-key --email <a
# founder>`: a key's permissions cannot be set over HTTP). It cannot decide proposals, edit entries
# or grant spend. A key minted before the grant-request change lacks hq_spend:request: mint a new one
# and revoke the old.
# HQ_AGENT_API_KEY=
# FACTORY_REPO is the repo the build pipeline works on (owner/repo). The build label MUST be agent:build
# and its branches agent/ (the defaults); the review label factory:review and deps/* never start a build.
# FACTORY_LABEL and FACTORY_BRANCH_PREFIX are set explicitly in a deploy (the code defaults equal these
# values); never set them to anything else.
# FACTORY_REPO=
# FACTORY_LABEL=agent:build
# FACTORY_BRANCH_PREFIX=agent/
# What a chat turn is assumed to cost until its real cost is known, in dollars (0 to 5; default 0.10).
# AGENT_TURN_ESTIMATE_USD=
# Slack: who may talk to the agent (workspace team ids, comma-separated; none listed, nobody does) and
# who may answer ANY approval in Slack (a repository write, the ship gate, a bigger model): eve
# principal ids such as slack:T01ABC:U01ABC, none listed, nobody can. These are the founders as the
# agent knows them, DERIVED from hq's founders table, never typed by hand: generate the value with
# `pnpm --filter @proof/hq founders:principals` and check a deploy with `... founders:principals --check`
# (it fails when this list and the table disagree). SLACK_BOT_TOKEN is read by eve itself;
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET (above) is read by the agent too, to verify a click before forwarding it to hq.
# SLACK_ALLOWED_TEAM_IDS=
# AGENT_FOUNDER_PRINCIPALS=
# The channel id (C01ABC) the nightly digest posts in. Unset, the digest posts nothing.
# SLACK_DIGEST_CHANNEL_ID=
# The org's GitHub App, for installation tokens: its id, its private key as a PEM (from the vault; one
# line with \n written out is fine) and the installation on the org. Never commit or log them.
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET is the secret the App signs its webhooks with (the GitHub channel verifies
# every delivery with it; unset, every delivery is refused). The vault holds one item per env name,
# each with a `credential` field, e.g. `op://<org>-factory/GITHUB_APP_ID/credential`. The four are
# GITHUB_APP_ID, GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID, GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY and GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID=
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Offline only: 1 runs the agent on scripted models for `pnpm --filter @proof/agent eval`. Refused in
# production and wherever SLACK_BOT_TOKEN is set. AGENT_PROBE_URL is set by that script, never by hand.
# AGENT_OFFLINE=
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
EMAIL_FROM="Proof <auth@example.com>"
# Auth emails go through SES via Wraps (us-east-1), with the AWS SDK's own credential chain. Locally,
# set AWS_PROFILE to an SSO profile whose account has EMAIL_FROM's domain verified, and leave
# DEV_MAIL_SINK unset. Read by the AWS SDK, not packages/env. See docs/auth.md, "Auth email locally".
# AWS_PROFILE=
# Optional: syncs signups to Wraps contacts
WRAPS_API_KEY=
# Optional: social sign-in. Each button appears only when both values are set. The callback URL is
# ${BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/github (or /callback/google).
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Optional: port for apps/api (defaults to the port in BETTER_AUTH_URL, then 3000).
# Under `pnpm dev`, portless sets PORT and PORTLESS_URL itself; apps/api then uses PORTLESS_URL as its URL.
PORT=
# Optional: fatal|error|warn|info|debug|trace|silent (default info; silent under test)
LOG_LEVEL=
# Requests per organization per UTC day while the owner has not verified their email (metering). Once
# claimed, the plan's limit applies (packages/plans/src/catalog.ts).
UNCLAIMED_ORG_DAILY_REQUEST_LIMIT=500
# Set to true only behind a proxy that overwrites X-Forwarded-For (signup rate limits use the client IP).
TRUST_PROXY=false
# Optional: vendors GET /status reports, from their own status pages (/health pings nothing). Comma-separated
# Name=https://status.example.com (an Atlassian Statuspage), Name=statusio:<page id> (status.io), or
# aws / aws@<region> / aws:<service>@<region> (AWS Health). Empty means the default: all of AWS,
# Cloudflare, Vercel, Neon and PlanetScale. Narrow it to what you use, e.g. aws:ses@us-east-1
STATUS_PROVIDERS=
# Optional: built web app to serve from this API origin (run `pnpm --filter @proof/app build` first).
WEB_DIST_DIR=
# Optional: origins the web app runs on when it is not the API's origin (comma-separated bare origins).
# Needed only then: auth callbacks (magic link, reset, social) and passkeys trust them. Under `pnpm dev` the web
# app is https://app.proof.localhost (portless.json); copy the URL portless prints. Leave empty in production, where apps/api serves the web app itself.
# e2e sets it to its web port.
WEB_ORIGINS=
# Dev only: where apps/app's vite dev server proxies API calls (default http://localhost:3000).
API_URL=
# apps/site build: the marketing site's own origin (canonical URLs, RSS) and the web app its
# "Sign in" and "Start free" link to. Defaults: http://localhost:4321 and the app's dev URL.
SITE_URL=
APP_URL=
# e2e only: model credential for `pnpm e2e` agent steps (Vercel AI Gateway). See e2e/README.md.
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
# Dev and e2e only: keep auth emails in memory instead of sending them; GET /api/dev/mail?to=<address> reads them.
# Refused when NODE_ENV=production.
DEV_MAIL_SINK=
# Auth rate limits (sign-in, mail, one-time codes) by client IP. "off" is for dev and e2e only; refused
# unless NODE_ENV is development or test.
AUTH_RATE_LIMIT=on
# Optional: Stripe test mode. Both are needed for billing to mount; a live (sk_live_) key is refused at startup.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Optional: plans billing offers, as JSON, e.g. [{"name":"pro","priceId":"price_..."}] from your Stripe test dashboard.
STRIPE_PLANS=
# Optional: the organization that runs the product. Only its members, keys and agents may change
# feature flag rollouts (docs/feature-flags.md). Its id is in whoami.
OPERATOR_ORGANIZATION_ID=
# Optional: support thread mail (docs/support.md). Agent replies are mailed to the requester with
# Reply-To support+<token>@SUPPORT_REPLY_DOMAIN (a domain whose mail reaches inbound, below).
# Unset, nothing is mailed. SUPPORT_EMAIL_FROM is the sender, verified in SES; unset, EMAIL_FROM.
SUPPORT_REPLY_DOMAIN=
SUPPORT_EMAIL_FROM=
# Optional: inbound support mail from Wraps (SES to S3, an EventBridge event posted to
# /api/auth/support/inbound with X-Wraps-Inbound-Key). Needs SUPPORT_REPLY_DOMAIN. Unset, off.
# `wraps email inbound status --reveal-secret` prints the key and the bucket.
WRAPS_INBOUND_KEY=
WRAPS_INBOUND_BUCKET=
WRAPS_INBOUND_REGION=
# Optional: inbound support mail from another relay (Postmark, Mailgun, a Cloudflare Email Worker)
# that POSTs the raw message to /api/auth/support/inbound with Authorization: Bearer <secret>.
# Needs SUPPORT_REPLY_DOMAIN; not with WRAPS_INBOUND_KEY. openssl rand -base64 32
SUPPORT_INBOUND_SECRET=
# Optional: support's timing. The delay before an agent's reply is mailed (ms; unset, 2 minutes) and
# how often the API runs every BYO sweep: delayed mail, solved tickets, notification emails (ms; unset, a minute).
SUPPORT_REPLY_DELAY_MS=
SUPPORT_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=
# Optional: the file store for uploads (avatars, organization logos). Unset, uploads are off.
# local (a directory this API serves at /files; for dev and single-server setups), s3, r2 or vercel-blob.
# See docs/storage.md for each one's setup.
STORAGE_DRIVER=
# s3 and r2: the bucket.
STORAGE_BUCKET=
# Where files are read from (a CDN or custom domain). Required for r2 (its r2.dev URL or your domain);
# s3 defaults to the bucket URL, local to <API URL>/files.
STORAGE_PUBLIC_URL=
# local: the directory files go in (default .storage, git-ignored).
STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR=
# s3: an IAM user's keys with s3:PutObject and s3:DeleteObject on the bucket. S3_SESSION_TOKEN only for
# temporary credentials; S3_ENDPOINT only for an S3-compatible server (MinIO, LocalStack).
S3_REGION=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_SESSION_TOKEN=
S3_ENDPOINT=
# r2: the Cloudflare account id and an R2 API token's S3 credentials (Object Read & Write on the bucket).
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# vercel-blob: the store's read-write token (Vercel sets it when you connect a Blob store to the project).
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=
# Optional: where product analytics go: log (the API's log), posthog or none. Unset, it is log when
# NODE_ENV=development and none otherwise. See docs/analytics.md.
ANALYTICS_PROVIDER=
# posthog: the project API key (phc_...) and, for EU cloud or self-hosting, the ingestion host
# (default https://us.i.posthog.com).
POSTHOG_API_KEY=
POSTHOG_HOST=
